  - [1mClassic script + global[0m — add [38;5;153msettings.js[39m, load it in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, expose something like
    [38;5;153mwindow.AppSettings[39m. Zero friction, still opens from [38;5;153mfile://[39m, nothing else changes. Cost: one more global, and the
    dependency is implicit in script order rather than declared — if someone reorders the tags, it breaks silently.
  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gets [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m, [38;5;153msettings.js[39m uses [38;5;153mexport[39m, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m uses [38;5;153mimport[39m. Real
    declared dependencies, no globals, and it's where the platform has landed. Cost: [38;5;153mfile://[39m stops working (module
    loading is CORS-restricted, so you need a local server to open the page), and it's a change to [38;5;153mindex.html[39m beyond
    adding a tag.

  Both are cheap to reverse — this is a two-file project. The real question is whether anyone opens [38;5;153mindex.html[39m directly
  from disk.

  [1mMy recommendation: ES modules.[0m The explicit [38;5;153mimport[39m is exactly what makes a settings module worth extracting — the
  dependency is visible in the file that uses it, not in tag ordering. The [38;5;153mfile://[39m restriction is the only real cost,
  and it's a [38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m away.

